---
scope: docs/commands/aide
intent: >
  docs/commands/aide/ is the canonical home of the five AIDE pipeline
  slash command templates — one per pipeline phase. Each file is the
  authoritative source text for one installed slash command, read at
  init time by scaffoldCommands and rendered byte-faithful into the
  host project's command directory under an `aide/` subfolder, so the
  host's slash commands are namespaced as `/aide:research`,
  `/aide:spec`, `/aide:build`, `/aide:qa`, and `/aide:fix`. Editing a
  template here is the only way to change what scaffoldCommands
  installs in every downstream host.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Exactly five template files, one per pipeline phase, named
      `research.md`, `spec.md`, `build.md`, `qa.md`, `fix.md`. The
      bare phase names exist because the enclosing `aide/` folder
      already carries the namespace — double-naming (`aide-research.md`)
      would duplicate the namespace at every reference and break the
      namespaced slash command syntax on the host side.
    - Each template is a self-contained checklist an agent can execute
      as a session protocol for its pipeline phase. It must be
      complete enough to run without the agent having read any other
      file — the installed host will not have the source repo
      available.
    - Template content is decision-form and action-oriented. Every
      checklist item is something an agent does, not something it
      reads about or considers. Prose that describes the phase
      without driving action is paraphrasing the spec, not extending
      it.
    - Installed on the host side at `<command-dir>/aide/<phase>.md`,
      so the agent invokes them as `/aide:research`, `/aide:spec`,
      etc. The namespace subfolder is load-bearing: it is what turns
      a flat command directory into a grouped, namespaced pipeline
      the host agent can discover by tab-completion.
  undesired:
    - A sixth template file added here without a corresponding
      pipeline phase in the canonical spec and a corresponding
      scaffoldCommands entry. File count here, phase count in the
      spec, and name list in scaffoldCommands must stay synchronized;
      a unilateral addition to any one of the three introduces
      silent drift.
    - Template filenames that reintroduce the `aide-` prefix the
      namespace already carries. A file called `aide-research.md`
      inside `aide/` is saying "aide" twice, and the host scaffold
      would install it as `aide/aide-research.md`, producing
      `/aide:aide-research` — a slash command nobody wanted.
    - Template content that paraphrases the canonical spec rather
      than directing action. A template is a checklist the agent
      runs; it is not a summary of what the phase means. If a
      checklist item reads like a description of the phase rather
      than a step in it, the item is wrong.
    - Templates that reference vault paths, brain notes, repository
      internals, or any resource the installed host cannot resolve.
      Once scaffolded, these files live in a host project with no
      connection to this repo. Every resource reference must be
      resolvable by the agent operating in the host.
    - A sibling folder at this level (`cursor/`, `windsurf/`, etc.)
      for host-specific variants. Pipeline phases are client-
      agnostic; the reason a host framework exists is orthogonal to
      what the phases do. Variants belong inside the templates as
      branching logic, not as parallel folder trees that multiply
      the three-way lock.
---

## Context

The AIDE pipeline is defined in the canonical spec as five phases.
An agent in a host project can read the methodology and understand
the phases, but understanding a phase is not the same as running it.
A running phase needs a session protocol: a checklist that tells the
agent what to do, in what order, and what counts as done. That is
what these templates provide.

Before this folder existed, the five templates lived as string
literals inside the init tooling. A maintainer who wanted to change
a checklist item had to edit source code, rebuild the server, and
release. Templates in a docs/ subfolder are editable Markdown — a
diff here propagates to every host on its next init run, without a
rebuild or release gate.

The namespace subfolder (`aide/`) exists because host frameworks
that support slash commands treat nested folders as namespace
prefixes. Installing `research.md` into `<command-dir>/aide/`
produces the slash command `/aide:research`. That namespacing keeps
the five pipeline commands visually grouped in tab completion, and
prevents collision with any other commands the host already defines
— a host with its own `build` command does not get its workflow
hijacked by the AIDE pipeline.

## Strategy

**The `aide/` folder is the namespace, not a prefix in the filename.**
Host frameworks derive slash command namespaces from folder nesting:
a file at `<command-dir>/aide/research.md` becomes `/aide:research`.
Putting the namespace in both the folder name and the filename
(`aide/aide-research.md`) would double the namespace, yielding
`/aide:aide-research` on the host. Folder-as-namespace is the only
correct layout, and the filenames must be bare phase names for the
result on the host side to read cleanly.

**One file per pipeline phase, named for the phase.** The template
for the research phase is `research.md`. Its filename signals that
unambiguously to any reader of the folder. Naming deviations — a
generic name, a phase abbreviation, a version suffix — force
readers to open files to understand the folder, which is the
failure mode progressive disclosure exists to prevent.

**Templates are checklists, not essays.** Each template is a
session protocol the agent runs top-to-bottom. The format is a
short context line and a checkbox list. Every item is imperative —
something the agent does. Explanatory prose belongs in the canonical
spec or the methodology block, not in the template; agents have
already been taught the phases before they run a template.

**Templates must be host-portable.** Once scaffolded, a template
file lives in a project with no connection to this repository, the
brain, or any external reference outside the host. Every resource a
checklist item names — a tool, a file type, a command — must exist
in or be computable from the host project. A checklist item that
says "see the canonical spec for details" is pointing at a resource
the host does not have.

**The three-way lock is mandatory.** File count here, phase count
in the canonical spec, and name list in scaffoldCommands must stay
synchronized. A change to one requires a change to all three in the
same commit. This is not a soft convention — it is the mechanical
guarantee that every phase the spec describes is runnable in every
host, and that no command installs a phase the spec doesn't
recognize.

## Good examples

A host project runs `aide_init`. Afterward, the developer types `/`
in their agent UI and sees the AIDE pipeline commands grouped under
a single namespace: `/aide:research`, `/aide:spec`, `/aide:build`,
`/aide:qa`, `/aide:fix`. The grouping is visual and alphabetical;
tab completion after typing `/aide:` shows the five phases and
nothing else. The pipeline is discoverable without documentation.

A research phase template (`research.md`) contains the checklist
item: "Search the brain, then external sources, then team notes,
in that order — stop when coverage is sufficient, not when all
sources are exhausted." The item is imperative, it directs a
specific action sequence, and it gives the agent a stopping
condition. An agent running `/aide:research` knows exactly what to
do and when it is done.

A fix phase template (`fix.md`) contains: "Fix ONE issue only. Do
not fix adjacent issues discovered during the session — add them
to the checklist instead." This is a protocol constraint that
exists to keep fix sessions atomic. It belongs in the template,
not in the spec, because it governs agent behavior during the
session, not the methodology's architecture.

## Bad examples

A template file named `aide-research.md` placed inside the `aide/`
folder. The host scaffold installs it at
`<command-dir>/aide/aide-research.md`, and the slash command
becomes `/aide:aide-research`. The namespace has been duplicated;
every invocation reads awkwardly and the developer has to type the
phase name twice. The fix is to rename the file to `research.md` —
the folder carries the namespace.

A template that opens with two paragraphs explaining why the
research phase matters and how it fits into the pipeline. The
agent running `/aide:research` has already received the methodology
block and the tool descriptions — this is the third time it has
been told what the phase is for. The template should start at the
first checklist item.

A QA template checklist item that reads: "Verify that the
implementation honors the spec's cascading intent model, including
all ancestor specs up the tree." The item is a description of a
methodology concept, not a step the agent takes. A correct item
says what the agent actually does: "Run aide_discover on the
target module to load the full spec chain from root to leaf
before comparing output."

A build template that references a team-internal wiki or a vault
note path. The host project that installs this template has no
access to either resource. The agent running `/aide:build` hits a
dead reference on the first try and loses trust in the pipeline.

A sibling folder `docs/commands/cursor/` added for Cursor-specific
command variants. The five pipeline phases have nothing to do with
which host framework is running them; the phase does what the
phase does regardless of editor. A parallel folder tree doubles
the three-way lock, forces every edit to ripple across duplicates,
and drifts silently the moment one side is updated without the
other.
